account / asset deletion lifecycle
==================================

Deferred out of the storage-quota work. These all depend on one coherent
lifecycle design and should be decided together, not piecemeal.

Related existing notes in `todo`: high priorities #8, #10, #11, #20;
subscription todo #4; schema todo #5.


1. b2 object deletion
---------------------
Permanent file/editor-asset objects are not deleted today; the upload sweeper
only deletes temporary source/editor upload objects. `DeleteFileWithOrphanedBlobs`
is the existing permanent-file pattern.

- `DeleteWorkspace` is a bare `DELETE FROM workspaces` relying on FK cascade,
  so files/materials/editor_assets rows vanish and their blobs are orphaned.
  Logical storage usage drops to zero while the bucket keeps billing.
- Editor assets have no orphan-collection path at all.
- Clones intentionally share `blob_path`, so deletion must stay
  reference-counted; `DeleteFileWithOrphanedBlobs` is the existing pattern and
  the cascade path needs an equivalent.
- Decide where deletion runs: inline (fails open, leaks on error) vs. a
  reaper job over a `pending_blob_deletions` table (durable, needs the table).
  The reaper is probably right, and it also covers the weekly
  "file content with no file record" sweep in schema todo #5.
- Interacts with the recycle bin (`todo` high priority #20): if deletion is
  soft for 30 days, decide whether soft-deleted bytes still count against the
  storage quota. Counting them is the honest answer since the bytes are still
  there, but it means a user can be blocked by content they think they deleted,
  so the UI has to show trashed usage separately.


2. downgrade / subscription expiry
----------------------------------
Storage quota is a creation gate, not a hard cap, so a pro user at 800 MiB who
drops to free just silently cannot create anything again until they delete
something. That is a bad experience and not a real policy.

- Needs: buffer period, warning mail + in-app notification on cancel, 3 days
  before deletion, and after deletion. Matches subscription todo #4 and
  high priority #11.
- Decide what happens to over-quota content at the end of the buffer: lock the
  workspace read-only, or perm-delete oldest-first, or require the user to
  choose. Locking is safer; deleting user data on a billing event needs an
  explicit, logged, consented path.
- No buffer period if the account is already deleted.
- Material revision retention already downgrades correctly on its own —
  `PruneMaterialRevisions` re-applies the current tier to every material, so a
  pro user dropping to free loses versions 8..30 on the next run. That is the
  intended behaviour but it is silent data loss on a billing event; it should
  at least be mentioned in the downgrade warning.
- Per-asset caps become tier-derived under the quota work, so after a downgrade
  a user may hold assets larger than their tier now permits. Existing assets
  stay; only new uploads are checked.


3. account deletion
-------------------
Covered in detail in `todo` high priority #11. The quota-specific parts:

- On soft-delete of a user (`is_deleted` flag, never a row delete), decide
  whether their `user_storage` row is zeroed, retained, or excluded from
  reconciliation. Retaining it is useful for reinstatement.
- Workspace content is charged to the workspace owner. If the owner is deleted
  but members remain, the charge target no longer exists. Either reassign
  ownership on delete, or make the workspace itself the accounting entity.
  This is the main open question and it should be settled before the quota
  ships, because `materials.owner_user_id` / `files.user_id` are denormalized
  and would need a rewrite pass if ownership becomes mutable.
- Active subscription must be cancelled before deletion is allowed.


4. ownership transfer
---------------------
There is no workspace ownership transfer today, which is the only reason the
denormalized owner columns in the quota work are safe. If transfer is ever
added it must rewrite `files.user_id`, `materials.owner_user_id`,
`upload_sessions.user_id`, `editor_assets.user_id`, and
`editor_asset_uploads.user_id`, and move the corresponding bytes between both
users' `user_storage` rows in one transaction. Cheapest guard: a test that
fails if a transfer path is introduced without touching the accounting code.
